# Photoreactivity examined through incorporation in metal-organic frameworks

## abstract
Metal-organic frameworks, typically built by bridging metal centres with organic linkers, have recently shown great promise for a wide variety of applications, including gas separation and drug delivery. Here, we have used them as a scaffold to probe the photophysical and photochemical properties of metal-diimine complexes. We have immobilized a M(diimine)(CO)(3)X moiety (where M is Re or Mn, and X can be Cl or Br) by using it as the linker of a metal-organic framework, with Mn(II) cations acting as nodes. Time-resolved infrared measurements showed that the initial excited state formed on ultraviolet irradiation of the rhenium-based metal-organic framework was characteristic of an intra-ligand state, rather than the metal-ligand charge transfer state typically observed in solution, and revealed that the metal-diimine complexes rearranged from the fac- to mer-isomer in the crystalline solid state. This approach also enabled characterization of the photoactivity of Mn(diimine)(CO)(3)Br by single-crystal X-ray diffraction.

## Photoreactivity examined through incorporation in metal2organic frameworks
Metal2organic frameworks, typically built by bridging metal centres with organic linkers, have recently shown great promise for a wide variety of applications, including gas separation and drug delivery. Here, we have used them as a scaffold to probe the photophysical and photochemical properties of metal2diimine complexes. We have immobilized a M(diimine) $(\mathsf{C O})_{3}\mathsf{X}$ moiety (where M is Re or Mn, and $\pmb{x}$ can be Cl or Br) by using it as the linker of a metal2organic framework, with Mn(II) cations acting as nodes. Time-resolved infrared measurements showed that the initial excited state formed on ultraviolet irradiation of the rhenium-based metal2organic framework was characteristic of an intra-ligand state, rather than the metal2ligand charge transfer state typically observed in solution, and revealed that the metal2diimine complexes rearranged from the fac- to mer-isomer in the crystalline solid state. This approach also enabled characterization of the photoactivity of Mn(diimine) $(C O)_{3}B r$ by single-crystal X-ray diffraction.
he study of metal2organic frameworks (MOFs) has developed dramatically over recent years due to the application of these materials in gas storage and separation1,2, catalysis3,4, photochemistry5–7, magnetism8,9 and even drug delivery10. The synthetic conditions used for the preparation of MOFs, including the types of cations and anions, metal–ligand ratio, temperature, solvent and reaction period, are understood to influence the kinetics and energetics of the growth of coordination polymers and can be tuned to produce stable porous structures under specific conditions11. MOFs are attractive targets for these applications due to the systematic approach used for their synthesis using Robson’s building-block methodology12. Such an approach provides the opportunity to incorporate species within the framework structure, facilitating specific functionality in defined environments. Recent studies have demonstrated the use of MOFs to stabilize unusual intermediates and the benefit of taking advantage of the unusual environment and the rigidity of the framework13,14. In particular, a number of mixed-metal MOFs have been synthesized15,16, including a functional iridium-containing coordination polymer that has been used as an $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ sensor in which the luminescence of the Ir unit is quenched in the presence of oxygen17.
In this article, we demonstrate how MOFs can be used to host photoactive coordination complexes as a building block of the framework, part of a new approach in the investigation of photophysical/ photochemical processes that makes particular use of $\mathrm{X}$ -ray crystallography. We have investigated the nature of the excited states and the formation of reactive intermediates for $\mathrm{Re(diimine)(CO)_{3}C l}$ and $\mathrm{Mn(diimine)(CO)_{3}X}$ ( $\mathrm{X=Br}$ , Cl) species by hosting the photoactive unit within a MOF. In particular we monitor CO loss and the photochemical isomerization process using infrared (IR) spectroscopy and X-ray crystallography. The $\mathrm{Re(diimine)(CO)_{3}C l}$ family of complexes has been investigated as photoluminescent probes for studying DNA damage18,19, solar energy applications including photocatalysis for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction20, as light absorbers for solar cells21,22, as luminescent components for supramolecular photochemistry23 and in electroluminescent devices24.
Although a few studies of photoactive species being incorporated into MOFs have been reported, the strategy here has not been used before and offers a variety of possible advantages over more conventional methods of studying photoactive centres. For example, photocrystallography is a rapidly growing technique because it allows the full three-dimensional structures of short-lived and metastable photoactivated species to be determined using X-ray diffraction methods25. One of the limitations is the relatively low conversion rate from the ground-state structure to the excited state—such a low conversion is likely to hinder the analysis of the excited-state components and may result from the generation of unfavourable strain energy within the crystal as the structure changes.
The incorporation of the photoactive unit within the structure of the MOF provides the photoactive centre with a very specific environment where the framework acts as a scaffold in which the photochemical reactions can occur without damaging the crystal. Additionally, the framework structure acts as a rigid matrix within which reactive intermediates may not have the potential for recombination reactions, thereby stabilizing any intermediate species. A further property of the MOF framework structure of potential use is the fact that it offers a unique environment that is essentially free from solvent molecules and, as such, may mimic the gas phase. This may be important for the elucidation of photophysical properties. In the condensed phase, solvents play a significant role in controlling the photophysical properties of excited states of transition metal–diimine complexes, with properties such as solvatochromism26–29 and rigidochromism30–33 demonstrating the importance of the environment around the photoactive molecule.
We describe the synthesis of a Mn–carboxylate MOF that contains the $\mathbf{M}(2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine) $\mathrm{(CO)}_{3}\mathrm{X}$ ( $\mathrm{\bf~M}=\mathrm{\bfRe},$ Mn; $\mathrm{X=Br}$ , Cl) moieties appended to the framework structure. The synthetic strategy we have used takes advantage of the concept of using ‘complexes as ligands’, which has been used in a variety of areas of supramolecular coordination chemistry34–36 and has recently been proposed as a strategy for introducing reactive metal centres into MOFs for application in catalysis and enhancing hydrogen storage16,37.
Importantly, the use of MOF single crystals to host photoactive centres allows for the possibility of definitive assessment of the localized structure of the photoactive centre before any excitation and potentially during or after the excitation. The design strategy used to create the MOF ensures that the local structural changes around the photoactive centre are unlikely to influence the overall framework structure. This has been achieved by means of the reaction of $\mathbf{M}(2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine- ${}_{5,5^{'}}$ -dicarboxylate) $(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{X}$ with $\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{acetate})_{2}$ to afford the three-dimensional MOF structures 1 (ReMn) and 2 $\mathbf{\Gamma}(\mathbf{MnMn})$ , which consist of the M(diimine)- complex as a linker and $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ cations as nodes. We describe experiments on 1 incorporated into a KBr disc to allow facile study by picosecond time-resolved infrared (TRIR) spectroscopy, elucidating the excited-state formation after UV photolysis. Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra of 1 in KBr and a solid-state powder attenuated total reflectance infrared (ATR-IR) study of 2 before and after UV photolysis is reported and demonstrates fac- to mer-isomerization. Finally, this result is repeated in a single-crystal photolysis experiment in which the crystal structures of 2 in the initial facisomer and the product mer-isomer are identified.
## Results and discussion
Compound 1 was prepared by the reaction of ${\mathrm{Re}}(2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine$^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid) $(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ with $\mathrm{{MnCl}}_{2}$ in $N,N^{\prime}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) by heating a solution of the two components to $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ Yellow single crystals of 1 were formed over a period of $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ and structural determination was carried out by single-crystal X-ray diffraction (Fig. 1). The X-ray structure confirms the formulation of 1, $\{\mathrm{Mn(DMF)_{2}[C_{12}H_{6}N_{2}O_{4}R e(C O)_{3}C l]\}_{\infty}}$ , and the fac-arrangement of the $\mathrm{Re(diimine)(CO)_{3}C l}$ moiety (Fig. 1a), which is usually observed in solution for the mononuclear Re–diimine complexes. Each $\mathrm{{Mn}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ centre is octahedrally coordinated to four O atoms from four separate carboxylate ligands, with two O-coordinated DMF molecules completing the coordination sphere. The O atoms from the carboxylate ligands adopt a bridging mode to connect the $\mathrm{{Mn}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ centres into chains along the crystallographic $a$ axis (Fig. 1b). The Mn-carboxylate chains are interconnected via the $\mathrm{Re(diimine)(CO)_{3}C l}$ moiety to form a three-dimensional framework structure. Thus the pyridyl rings of the ligands sit perpendicular to the crystallographic $c$ axis, and the resulting channels revealed down the $a$ axis are occupied by the Re-coordinated carbonyl groups and chloride anions together with the Mn-coordinated DMF molecules. Both the $\mathrm{{Mn}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ cations and the Re(diimine) complexes act as four-connected nodes within the structure, each with $4^{2}.8^{4}$ Schlafli vertex symbols such that the overall framework adopts a pts topology38. The extended framework 1 was found to be stable in air and even at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for more than 2 days.
The fac- $.{\mathrm{Re}}({\mathrm{bpy}})({\mathrm{CO}})_{3}{\mathrm{Cl}}$ moiety has recently been shown to undergo photoinduced CO loss and subsequent isomerization to form the mer-isomer on irradiation with higher-energy UV light in solution44. We have investigated the UV photolysis of 1 in a KBr disc at $200~\mathrm{K}$ and monitored the reaction using FTIR spectroscopy. The IR difference spectrum obtained after $22\mathrm{h}$ of irradiation at $200\mathrm{K}$ is shown in Fig. 3. The paren $\nu(\mathrm{CO})$ bands are clearly bleached, and several new absorptions can be seen, including a weak band at ${\sim}2,132\ c m^{-1}$ due to the production of free CO in the matrix. Once formed, the difference spectrum remains unchanged until the sample is warmed. A higher energy band at $2{,}043\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is clearly observed. There are also new features at $\sim1,810\ c m^{-1}$ and between 1,850 and $1,950\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , overlapping with the ground state. The FTIR spectra were fitted using Lorentzian line-shapes, affording thre $\nu(\mathrm{CO})$ bands at 2,043, 1,925 and $1,868~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which can be assigned to $\upnu(\mathrm{CO})$ bands of the merisomer by comparison with recent studies on $[\mathrm{Re(bpy)(CO)}_{3}\mathrm{Cll}]$ (ref. 44). Other bands at 1,893, 1,855, 1,813 and $1,794~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are present. Also, cis,trans- $\mathrm{[Re(bpy)(CO)_{2}(C D_{3}C N)C l]}$ (1,906 and $\mathrm{\dot{1}}{,}828\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) and cis,cis- $[\mathrm{Re(bpy)(CO)}_{2}(\mathrm{CD}_{3}\mathrm{CN)Cl]}$ (1,902 and
$1,824~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ ) have been reported previously in $\mathrm{CD}_{3}\mathrm{CN}_{:}$ , and the bands assigned at 1,893, 1,855, 1,813 and $1{,}794\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$ due to the presence of two CO loss products. On warming to above $\sim250\mathrm{K},$ the bands assigned to the CO loss products decay and the bands of the fac- and mer-isomers increase in intensity.
The photochemical conversion of the fac-Re(diimine)(CO)Cl to mer-Re(diimine)(CO)Cl within the framework can be demonstrated by imaging the conversion in the entire KBr disc. Figure 3 shows the optical image of the KBr disc $\mathrm{\stackrel{.}{\sim}}4\times4\mathrm{mm}\mathrm{\rangle}$ containing the ReMn MOF after warming to room temperature. The disc is not circular, because it was trimmed to fit the low-temperature FTIR matrix cell. The FTIR false colour image of the KBr disc is shown, obtained using the ratio of the $\nu(\mathrm{CO})$ bands of the mer- to fac-isomer bands. It is clear that the FTIR image accurately represents the optical image, with the outer boundary of the disc being clearly defined. The green area represents the MOF containing a high concentration of fac-Re(diimine) $\mathrm{(CO)}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ and the blue region shows areas outside the disc where there is no Re–diimine present. The FTIR image obtained before photolysis confirms that 1 is evenly distributed throughout the KBr (not shown). After photolysis there is a high intensity of mer-Re(diimine) $\mathrm{(CO)}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ (red region) in the centre of the disc. This corresponds to the area that was photolysed, as the outer rim of the disc is masked by the cell used for photolysis. The conversion of fac- to $m e r{-}\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{diimine})(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ is uniform within the photolysed region of the disc. The small circular imperfection in the mapped area is due to a hole in the FTIR imaging stage designed to allow transmission spectra to be recorded. Because this map was recorded using reflectance imaging, the hole significantly affects the signal recorded directly above it. Once formed, the mer-isomer is stable in the solid state at room temperature. Attempts to monitor this by X-ray crystallography were unsuccessful, presumably due to low conversion, which was estimated to be ${\sim}10\%$ . We have therefore investigated the analogous structure containing a $\mathrm{Mn(bpy)(CO)_{3}C l}$ moiety, because such complexes are known to have a higher quantum yield of CO loss and fac to mer isomerization45.
Single crystals of 2a, a material structurally analogous to 2, were successfully prepared by reaction of the Mn(diimine) $\mathrm{\bar{(CO)}}_{3}\mathrm{Br}$ linker with $\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}$ . Unfortunately, this method resulted in a large degree of ‘scrambling’ of the halides, making definitive assignment of the halide difficult; however, crystals were found to be of an adequate standard to be analysed by single-crystal X-ray diffraction. On the basis of CHN analysis, the Mn-bound halide was found to be predominantly chloride rather than bromide, and the singlecrystal structures were refined accordingly. Attempts to prepare a chloro-analogue of 2 by reaction of Mn(diimine) $\mathrm{(CO)}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ with $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{acetate})_{2}$ were performed, but resulted in decomposition of the $\mathrm{Mn(diimine)(CO)_{3}C l}$ moiety under the conditions used.
Single-crystal X-ray diffraction studies of crystal of 2a confirmed the fac arrangement of the Mn(diimine) $(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ moiety and the same overall topology of the MOF as observed for 1. The poor quality of the crystals meant that data of sufficient quality could only be obtained using an intense synchrotron X-ray source. A single crystal of 2a in oil was cooled with dry ice and irradiated for $40\:s$ with a 300 W Xe lamp to give 2b by inducing isomerization of the $\mathrm{Mn(diimine)(CO)_{3}C l}$ from the fac to mer isomer. Photoisomerization was confirmed by single-crystal X-ray diffraction, with the appearance of electron density consistent with a partially occupied chloride ligand in equatorial sites of the Mn(diimine) $\mathrm{(CO)}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ moiety (Fig. 5), demonstrating fac to mer isomerization despite degradation of crystal quality due to the photoexcitation process. These observations have a wider impact, particularly in fast time-resolved X-ray studies, which is an area of intense activity because this is the only technique that allows the determination of geometry changes to be made when molecules within a crystal lattice are excited, and it provides a full three-dimensional picture of the excited molecule25. However, widespread application of this powerful technique to probe the structure and follow the dynamics of small molecules is often limited by crystal breakdown. Our new crystal environment offers the potential to reduce the problem of laser breakdown by allowing structural changes to occur within the MOF framework without fundamentally altering the integrity of the crystal lattice. This opens up such an approach to study a wide range of small molecules.
## Conclusions
The examples reported here illustrate the power of the strategy of using MOFs as hosts for guest photoactive species. This approach opens up the possibility of using MOF hosts to stabilize typically short-lived species, prolonging lifetimes and potentially giving enhanced insight into the nature of short-lived intermediate species. Our studies are currently pursuing these goals and we anticipate significant advances in understanding the nature of photoactive species using the MOF-based strategy.
